<section id="whyone" class="main special">
	<div class="container">
		<span class="image fit primary"><img src="images/shadow.jpg" alt="" /></span>
		<div class="content">
            <p><h5>Why train with an independent?</h5></p>
            <p>
                Why train part 61? For a while when the airlines were desperate for pilots training part 141 had a small advantage. Pilots qualified for ATP
                kicenses at 1200 total flight hours instead of the 1500 hours required with other training. People looking for an airline career could get a jump
                by training in a part 141 program. This is no longer the case though. airline hiring has returned to normal and to get considered for a job most
                pilots will need 2000 hours or more.
            </p>
            <p>
                Part 141 training also had big disadvantages. Since students were charged a flat fee, part 141 programs are incentivized to get you in and out
                with the minimum hours in their airplanes possible. This often leads to check ride failures which are a permanent black mark on your record.
                Also minimum flight time is not what you want. The chance of getting hired at 250 hours flight time is basically non existant.
            </p>			
		</div>
		<a href="#whytwo" class="goto-next scrolly">Next</a>
	</div>
</section>

